--TEST--
A certificate name's value that holds a special character reads back escaped as RFC 4514 § 2.4 escapes it, so a one-attribute name never reads as the same text as a name with two — `rule:testing/a-failing-proof-is-fixed-or-recorded`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Http\TlsInfo::subject
// covers: Core\Http\TlsInfo::issuer
// `CN=x+O=y` is one common name whose value is `x+O=y`, so its `+` is escaped.
var $one = Core\Test::tlsSession({subject: "CN=x+O=y", issuer: "CN=#1 \"CA\"; <a> b\\c"});
echo $one->subject(), "\n";
echo $one->issuer(), "\n";

// A plain name reads back exactly as it was written.
var $plain = Core\Test::tlsSession({subject: "CN=api.example.com, O=Shop"});
echo $plain->subject(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
CN=x\+O=y
CN=\#1 \"CA\"\; \<a\> b\\c
CN=api.example.com, O=Shop
